    id: 'todai-tokyo-stock-exchange-longer-hours',
    title: 'Should a Stock Market Stay Open Longer?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "On November 5, 2024, the Tokyo Stock Exchange extended its trading day by thirty minutes. Shares had previously stopped changing hands at three o'clock in the afternoon, and trading now continues until half past three. The change was reportedly the first extension of the exchange's hours in about seventy years. To an outsider, half an hour may seem a small matter, but it was the result of years of discussion. It also raised a broader question that markets around the world have been asking: how long should a stock market stay open?",
      "Before the change, the Tokyo market was open for five hours a day, from nine until half past eleven in the morning and from half past twelve until three. The lunch break was kept, so the new schedule offers five and a half hours of trading. The longer afternoon was introduced together with a new system of closing prices. In the last five minutes of the day, orders are collected without being matched, and at the close they are all executed at a single price. Such closing auctions were already common on several large exchanges abroad.",
      "Supporters argue first that the extra time makes the market more resilient. In October 2020, a fault in the exchange's computer system stopped trading in all shares for an entire day. Under the old schedule, a problem that was not fixed by early afternoon left almost no time to restart, so the whole day's trading could be lost. With a longer afternoon, the exchange has a better chance of reopening on the same day. Supporters also say that the closing auction produces a more reliable final price, which matters to funds that buy and sell at the close, such as those that track a stock index.",
      "Their second argument concerns competition and convenience. A market that closes early gives investors less time to react to news, and investors elsewhere in Asia have a shorter period in which their working hours overlap with Tokyo's. Supporters note that the main market in New York trades for six and a half hours without a break. In their view, a market that wants to attract investment from around the world must be open when that investment is ready to arrive. Some even argue that the lunch break should be abolished as well.",
      "Critics, however, point to the costs. Every extra half hour of trading must be supported by staff at securities companies, by the systems that check and settle trades, and by people who prepare reports after the close. For smaller firms in particular, these costs are not trivial. Employees who already work long hours may be asked to stay later still. Critics also doubt that longer hours will ( A ) much new trading. In their view, investors who have a fixed amount of money to trade may simply spread the same orders over a longer day.",
      "A second concern is the timing of company announcements. Many listed companies have traditionally released their results at about three o'clock, just as trading ended, which gave investors the evening to study the figures before they could act. Under the new schedule, an announcement made at that time arrives while trading is still under way, unless the company chooses to wait. Critics fear that professional traders using fast computers will then react within seconds and gain a larger advantage over ordinary investors, who may be at work and unable to follow the news. They argue that the length of the trading day is not simply a question of convenience but also one of fairness.",
      "In my view, the extension of 2024 was a sensible step. The protection it offers against a lost day of trading is a real benefit, and the closing auction brings Tokyo into line with practice elsewhere. However, I am not convinced that longer is always better. Before any further extension is considered, the exchange should publish evidence on how the extra half hour is actually used, by whom and at what cost. A market should stay open for as long as it serves investors well, and that should be decided by evidence rather than by a race with other exchanges.",
    ],
